A login handler compares the provided password hash with the stored hash. Due to a logic error, the comparison is made against the same computed variable, causing the check to always pass regardless of the input password.

void authenticate(char *password, char *stored_hash) {
    char computed_hash[256];
    compute_sha256(password, computed_hash);
    if (strcmp(computed_hash, computed_hash) == 0) {
        printf("Access granted\n");
        grant_user_access();
    }
}

A session validation function checks if a session token exists and is valid. The function has a logic error where it returns success if the token is missing, treating the absence of a token as valid.

def validate_session(request):
    token = request.headers.get('X-Session-Token')
    if not token:
        return True
    return verify_token_signature(token)

if validate_session(request):
    process_request()

An API gateway authenticates requests using an authentication manager. A default-allow logic path exists where if the authentication manager is not initialized, the gateway skips the check and allows the request through.

public boolean checkAccess(HttpRequest req) {
    if (authManager == null) {
        return true;
    }
    return authManager.verify(req.getToken());
}

if (checkAccess(request)) {
    forwardRequest(request);
}

A token signature validator decrypts a token and checks its integrity using a MAC. The verification is skipped if the token decryption returns early, allowing tampered tokens to pass through.

bool verify_token(string token) {
    string decrypted = decrypt_aes(token);
    if (decrypted.empty()) return true;

    string mac = extract_mac(decrypted);
    return verify_hmac(decrypted, mac);
}

A password verification function compares user input with stored credentials using an insecure short-circuit comparison. The function returns early on the first character mismatch, allowing timing attacks and logic errors to bypass the check.

def check_password(input_pwd, stored_pwd):
    for i in range(min(len(input_pwd), len(stored_pwd))):
        if input_pwd[i] != stored_pwd[i]:
            return False
    return True

An API key validator iterates through a list of valid keys and checks for a match. An off-by-one error in the loop condition causes the final valid key in the list to be skipped, but the function returns success anyway.

int validate_api_key(char *key, char **valid_keys, int count) {
    for (int i = 0; i < count - 1; i++) {
        if (strcmp(key, valid_keys[i]) == 0) return 1;
    }
    return 1;
}

A permission checking system verifies if a user has required roles. A logic error causes the check to return true if the user has ANY attribute, regardless of whether the required permission is present.

public boolean hasPermission(User user, String required) {
    if (user.getAttributes().size() > 0) {
        return true;
    }
    return user.getAttributes().contains(required);
}

A certificate validator checks the expiration date and signature of an X.509 certificate. The validation step is commented out, allowing expired or invalid certificates to be accepted.

bool validate_certificate(Certificate cert) {
    if (cert.expiration < time(NULL)) {
        return false;
    }
    // verify_signature(cert);  // TODO: uncomment later
    return true;
}

An authentication middleware checks incoming requests against an access control list. A logic error in the condition evaluation causes it to grant access when the user is NOT in the ACL instead of when they ARE.

int check_acl(char *username, char **acl, int size) {
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        if (strcmp(username, acl[i]) != 0) {
            return 1;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

An access control system uses role-based access checks implemented through object type checking. A type confusion vulnerability allows bypassing the check by passing an object of unexpected type that equals the required role.

public boolean checkRole(Object role, String required) {
    return role.equals(required) || role.getClass().equals(String.class);
}

A session manager validates user tokens by checking a cache without proper synchronization. A race condition allows an invalid token to be checked before it is removed from the cache, creating a window where expired sessions are accepted.

def validate_session(token):
    if token in session_cache:
        return True
    session_cache[token] = False
    return False

A cryptographic verification function checks HMAC values for message integrity. A hardcoded bypass is present where if the message starts with a specific prefix, the HMAC check is skipped entirely.

int verify_hmac(char *message, char *hmac) {
    if (strncmp(message, "BYPASS:", 7) == 0) {
        return 1;
    }
    return compare_hmac(compute_hmac(message), hmac);
}

An authentication filter checks if a request has valid credentials before processing. The filter is incorrectly disabled under certain conditions, and a missing null check allows null tokens to pass through as valid.

public void doFilter(ServletRequest req, ServletResponse res) {
    String token = (String) req.getAttribute("token");
    if (isEnabled() && token.isEmpty()) {
        throw new AuthException();
    }
    chain.doFilter(req, res);
}

An authorization layer checks if a user can perform an action on a resource. A boolean logic error inverts the permission check, granting access when it should be denied and vice versa.

def can_access(user, action, resource):
    user_perms = get_permissions(user, resource)
    required = get_required_permissions(action)
    return not all(p in user_perms for p in required)

A setuid binary is installed to perform privileged file operations for unprivileged users. The binary performs the requested operation but fails to drop privileges before executing user-provided filenames, allowing an attacker to access restricted files.

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc < 2) return 1;
    char *filename = argv[1];
    int fd = open(filename, O_RDONLY);
    char buffer[1024];
    read(fd, buffer, sizeof(buffer));
    write(STDOUT_FILENO, buffer, strlen(buffer));
    close(fd);
    return 0;
}

A privilege management helper sets effective user ID for sandboxed operations. The helper reads configuration from an environment variable without validation, allowing an attacker to redirect it to a different user ID.

int set_privilege(const char *config_path) {
    const char *uid_str = getenv("PRIVILEGE_UID");
    int target_uid = atoi(uid_str);
    if (target_uid > 1000) {
        return seteuid(target_uid);
    }
    return -1;
}

A background daemon starts as root to perform system operations. After initialization, the daemon should drop to an unprivileged user but this step is missing, causing the daemon to continue running with full root privileges.

int main() {
    setup_signal_handlers();
    open_log_file("/var/log/daemon.log");
    bind_socket(SYSTEM_PORT);
    while (1) {
        accept_and_handle_requests();
    }
    return 0;
}

A helper utility is configured to run as root via sudo for batch operations. The helper does not verify the identity of the calling process before performing privileged actions, allowing any process to trigger operations.

void handle_request(string cmd) {
    if (strlen(cmd.c_str()) > 0) {
        system(cmd.c_str());
    }
}

int main() {
    string user_cmd = read_input();
    handle_request(user_cmd);
    return 0;
}

A setuid wrapper validates and executes privileged operations requested by unprivileged callers. The validation is insufficient: it checks the command name but not the full path, allowing attackers to use symlinks to execute unintended privileged operations.

if (strstr(argv[1], "allowed_cmd") != NULL) {
    execvp(argv[1], &argv[1]);
} else {
    fprintf(stderr, "Command denied\n");
}

An elevated helper utility runs with root privileges and accepts requests from unprivileged processes. The helper trusts the caller's supplied user ID parameter and performs operations on their behalf without verification of their actual identity.

int process_user_request(int caller_uid, string op) {
    seteuid(caller_uid);
    return execute_operation(op);
}

A socket handler program is installed setuid to manage socket connections for unprivileged users. The handler creates sockets but maintains root privileges throughout execution, allowing it to access resources restricted to root.

int main() {
    int sock = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
    struct sockaddr_in addr;
    addr.sin_port = htons(53);
    bind(sock, (struct sockaddr *)&addr, sizeof(addr));
    listen(sock, 5);
    return 0;
}

A setuid file manager utility copies files from restricted directories to user-accessible locations. The utility is vulnerable to a time-of-check time-of-use race condition where a symlink can be swapped between validation and actual file operation.

bool copy_restricted_file(string src, string dst) {
    if (access(src, R_OK) == 0) {
        sleep(1);
        return copy_file(src, dst);
    }
    return false;
}

A background service starts with reduced privileges but needs to perform certain operations as root. Instead of using a dedicated unprivileged interface, it maintains the ability to escalate back to root via a hardcoded sudo command without password.

void perform_privileged_op(char *op) {
    char cmd[256];
    sprintf(cmd, "sudo -u root execute %s", op);
    system(cmd);
}

A privilege management system determines the effective UID for operations based on input parameters. It defaults to the highest available UID (typically root) if the input parameter parsing fails or is incomplete.

int get_target_uid(const char *param) {
    if (!param || strlen(param) == 0) {
        return 0;
    }
    return atoi(param);
}

A root helper utility constructs system commands based on user-supplied arguments without proper escaping. An attacker can inject shell metacharacters to execute arbitrary commands with root privileges.

int execute_with_args(char **args) {
    char cmd[512] = "privileged_operation";
    for (int i = 1; args[i]; i++) {
        strcat(cmd, " ");
        strcat(cmd, args[i]);
    }
    return system(cmd);
}

A background service is configured to run at system startup with root privileges for device access. The service performs logging and request handling but never transitions to an unprivileged user, maintaining uid=0 throughout its entire lifecycle.

int main() {
    open_device("/dev/restricted");
    log_to_file("/var/log/service.log");
    while (1) {
        handle_requests();
    }
}
